>> title: Winter Melon and Job's Tears Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, winter melon, job's tears, cantonese, old-fire soup, summer, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 冬瓜薏米排骨湯, dung gwaa ji mai paai gwat tong, dung gwa yi mai pai gwat tong, winter melon and job's tears soup, winter melon and Chinese barley pork rib soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr 30 min
>> slack: forgiving — peeling the melon loses the pot, because the skin is the reason the melon is in it at all
>> step.1: 祛濕 — the summer pot. The skin stays on the melon and the seeds stay in: that is where the property sits.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the bones
>> step.3: soak the dried goods 30 min
>> step.4: simmer 3 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.6: The broth is the dish. The solids are spent.

The summer pot. 祛濕 is the word — the damp season — and the melon and the job's tears are both in it for that.

Blanch @pork ribs{1 1/2%lb}(680 g; cut into single ribs) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse the ribs under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Soak @raw job's tears{1/3%cup}(saang ji mai; the raw kind, in for 祛濕) and @toasted job's tears{1/3%cup}(suk ji mai; the toasted kind, in for the roundness — the two go in together and neither stands in for the other) with @dried scallops{3}(jiu cyu; the soaking water goes in with them, and it is the most savoury thing in the bowl) in cold water, ~soak{30%min}, then lift them out and leave the grit behind.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched ribs{} with @&(~1)soaked goods{}, @winter melon{2%lb}(900 g; skin on, seeds in, in thick wedges), @ginger{3%slices} and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{3%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish.
